Computing $\sin a,\sin b,$ and $\cos a \sin b + \sin a \cos b.$</think> <answer> 
\begin{align*}
\sin a \overline{=} \frac{4}{5} \qquad \sin b \overline{=} \frac{12}{13} \qquad \cos a \sin b + \sin a \cos b &\overline{=} \frac{63}{65} 
\end{align*}
We apply the identity $\cos^2 \theta = 1-\sin^2 \theta$ to find $\cos a = \frac{3}{5}$ and $\cos b = \frac{5}{13}$ . $(\because$ $a$ and $b$ are acute angles $)$ So $\cos (a + b) = \cos a \cos b - \sin a \sin b = \frac{3}{5} \cdot \frac{5}{13} - \frac{4}{5} \cdot \frac{12}{13} = \boxed{-\frac{33}{65}}$.
</answer>

